# Project Rules

## Project Shape
- This project is configured for SwarmForge with four Claude-backed agents: specifier, coder, refactorer, and architect.
- Project language: Ruby (Ruby 3.3 via rbenv), Rails 8.1.
- Scorekeepr is an event-sourced SaaS for game leagues. There is **no
  ActiveRecord**: the only persistence is the append-only `events` table of
  the DCB event store (github.com/Kjeldahl/ruby-dcb).

## Canonical Documentation (read before changing anything)
- `docs/DOMAIN.md`: domain rules and scoring model. **Normative.** Never
  contradict it.
- `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`: slice conventions, the routing table, and the hard
  constraints. The routing table and `config/routes.rb` must stay in sync.
- `docs/REVIEW.md`: the "adding a new slice" checklist.

## Hard Constraints
- Vertical slices under `app/slices/<slice>/{domain,web,views}`. Slices never
  reference each other's classes. **Events are the only cross-slice
  contract** (sole exception: `ApplicationController` to `Identity::Users.find`).
  Shared UI lives in `app/views/layouts` and
  `app/controllers/application_controller.rb` only.
- Events are immutable past-tense facts. All invariants are enforced via DCB
  append conditions: every command append carries its decision model's append
  condition when an invariant depends on what was read; failed validation never
  writes. No read-then-write without a condition. Read models are projections
  with no hidden state outside the event store.
- Dependency direction is web to domain to EventStore. Controllers contain no
  domain logic.
- Rejection messages in commands must match the feature files verbatim.

## Environment
- The gem bindir may be missing from PATH. Run tools as
  `PATH=/opt/rbenv/versions/3.3.6/bin:$PATH bundle exec ...`.
- Tests default to the in-memory event store (parallel-safe). Postgres is only
  needed for the dev server and for the integration path
  (`EVENT_STORE_ADAPTER=postgres bundle exec rspec/cucumber`).
- Demo data: `bin/rails demo:seed` (or `demo:populate` for larger sets).

## Acceptance Pipeline
- Acceptance is **Cucumber**: `bin/rails quality:features`, with `features/*.feature`
  and step definitions per slice. Do **not** install or build the APS
  `gherkin-parser`/`gherkin-mutator` tooling or a separate acceptance
  entrypoint generator. Cucumber is the pipeline for now.
- There is no Gherkin acceptance mutation in this project. Do not run it.

## Quality Gates (all must stay green - use rake tasks with `sh_logged`)
- `bin/rails quality:spec`: unit specs (mutation-tested).
- `bin/rails quality:features`: the behaviour spec.
- `bin/rails quality:rubocop`: omakase style.
- `bin/rails quality:packwerk`: no cross-slice constant references.
- `bin/rails quality:crap`: crap4r, **CRAP threshold 8** (exit 0 required).
- `bin/rails quality:mutant`: mutant, 100% domain coverage (incremental;
  `quality:mutant:full` for the full run). Use **jobs 4** on the in-memory
  store; **jobs 1** if you force `EVENT_STORE_ADAPTER=postgres`.
- `bin/rails quality:dry`: dry4r (github.com/Kjeldahl/dry4r). Use it to reduce
  duplication where reasonable. There is no separate property-testing framework;
  add property tests only when explicitly requested.
- `bin/rails quality:zeitwerk`: autoload consistency check.

## Self-Logging Rake Tasks
- Every quality rake task self-logs via the shared Rakefile helpers
  (`run_logged`/`sh_logged`): it tees full output to a timestamped file under
  `tmp/logs/` (e.g. `tmp/logs/mutant-2026-05-21T14:43:34.log`) and prints
  `log: tmp/logs/<file>` as the last line of stdout. Keep this in the shared
  helpers so every task inherits it - callers add no shell redirection of their
  own.
- To inspect results, read or grep the printed log file. Never wrap a task in
  shell redirection, `tee`, or a logging subagent, and never re-run a task just
  to read a single number.
- For threshold gates (crap4r CRAP, mutant coverage), parse the metric from the
  logged file rather than trusting exit status.

## Local Configuration
- Preserve project-local SwarmForge configuration under `swarmforge/`.
- Keep swarm state local under `.swarmforge/`, worktrees under `.worktrees/`,
  and shared scripts under `swarmforge/scripts/`.

## Handoffs
- Prefer terse, explicit handoffs that report state and request role-appropriate
  review. Do not include verifications or sender process narrative.

## Ownership
- Do not change another role's prompt or workflow ownership without explicit
  user direction.
